 <iati-activity default-currency="EUR" hierarchy="2" humanitarian="0" iati-activities:version="2.02" xml:lang="EN" xmlns:iati-activities="http://d-portal.org/xmlns/iati-activities" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
  <iati-identifier>NL-KVK-40409352-PRJ13-132-0009</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="NL-KVK-40409352" type="21">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Netherlands Red Cross</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Paratus</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Based on a thorough analysis of  the  current  risk situation,  and  ongoing  trends,  scenarios  of  multi-hazard  impacts  are  co-designed  with  stakeholders,  and developed in four case study areas (Alpine transportation corridors, Romanian civil protection, earthquake threats  to  the  megacity  of  Istanbul,  and  impact-based  forecasting  related  to  tropical  cyclone  threats  and other compounding events to preparedness planning in the Caribbean).  The project aims to develop a cloud-based  simulation  and  information  service  building  on  results  from  previous  EU  projects  with guidelines on approaches,  best  practices,  data  sources  and  analysis  tools  for  risk  assessment,  simulation  of  possible  scenarios  and  decision  support  tools  for  the  evaluation  of  optimal  risk  mitigation/adaptation  measures. Through the use of participatory planning tools, and serious games, stakeholder will evaluate the options for risk reduction to support technical policy improvements and implementation of actions,  considering possible scenarios of   changing   hazards, exposure and vulnerability.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Caribbean region, Alps mountain region, Romania,, Turkey/Istanbul.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The   PARATUS   project   aims   to   develop a   service   for   multi-hazard   systemic   vulnerability  and  dynamic  risk  scenarios  that  considers  the  uncertainties  of  the  risk  components,  and  supports    the  evaluation  of  adaptation  measures  with  and  for  first  and  second  responders,  and  other  stakeholders.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Covid-19 pandemic and climate crisis have taught us hard lessons about the systemic impact of compounding disasters. Stakeholders in disaster risk management are faced with the challenge to adapt their risk reduction policies and emergency plans but lack the tools to account for the cross-sectoral impacts and dynamic nature of the risks involved.</narrative>
  </description>
